<div class="page-head">
  <div class="page-head-row page-head-row--split page-head-row--stacked">
    <h1 class="section-title"><span class="gradient-text">JEV</span> — decision sidecar</h1>
    <%= link_to "Run Job", new_job_path, class: "btn btn-primary head-action" %>
    <%# The conf preview sits under Run Job, in the same slot the JSON page uses
        for its generated-spec popup. Both are "here is the thing that will be
        sent" views of a page, so they get the same treatment. %>
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary head-action" id="jevConfBtn"
            title="Jev conf" aria-label="Jev conf">Jev conf</button>
    <p class="page-head-blurb">An optional second opinion from TypeSafe's System One model. It reads the measurements from a proof and answers four narrow questions, one per failure signature. The answers fold into a verdict — and that verdict never overrides the deterministic gates or picks a candidate.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<%#
  The four questions. Each section's EXAMPLES became a popup behind its "?", so
  the page reads as six short cards instead of twenty-two example cards, and the
  vocabulary is where you go when you want it.

  The "?" sits in the card-head beside the title -- the same slot and the same
  .help-btn as the JSON page's cards -- and its accessible name contains the
  visible title (WCAG 2.5.3).
%>
<% questions = [
  {
    key: "failure_mode", type: "choice", title: "failure_mode",
    blurb: "Which failure signature best describes this candidate — judged only from the preflight measurements and finding details.",
    examples: [
      ["screen_explosion", "Far more colours than allowed"],
      ["continuous_tone", "Smooth tonal ramp — unprintable flat"],
      ["geometry_overload", "Too many nodes. Slow to RIP"],
      ["palette_mismatch", "Colours fall outside the palette"],
      ["render_or_tool_failure", "The proof or PDF failed"],
      ["no_hard_failure", "No hard finding"],
      ["unclear", "No single clear signature"]
    ]
  },
  {
    key: "print_route", type: "choice", title: "print_route",
    blurb: "Which production route fits this artwork as measured.",
    examples: [
      ["screen_spot_low", "Spot colour, within budget"],
      ["screen_spot_multi", "Spot colour, over budget"],
      ["process_halftone", "4-colour process — tone must survive"],
      ["dtg_or_dtf", "DTG or DTF fits better"],
      ["unsuitable", "No route fits without rework"],
      ["unclear", "Route not determined"]
    ]
  },
  {
    key: "tonal_structure", type: "score", title: "tonal_structure",
    blurb: "How faithfully the trace preserved the source's tonal structure — judged only from measured colour count, ramp and coverage numbers.",
    examples: [
      ["Broken", "Tone collapsed into noise"],
      ["Flat", "A few hard steps, visible banding"],
      ["Retained", "Coherent read in flat colour"],
      ["Engraving-grade", "Dense, ordered, deliberate"]
    ]
  },
  {
    key: "evidence_is_actionable", type: "noul", title: "evidence_is_actionable",
    blurb: "Do the supplied measurements contain enough reliable evidence to act on a production decision, given their stated limitations?",
    examples: [
      ["true", "Source, render and PDF all present"],
      ["false", "A required measurement is missing"]
    ]
  }
] %>

<div class="grid-2 jev-sections">
  <% questions.each do |q| %>
    <div class="card jev-section">
      <div class="card-head">
        <h3><%= q[:title] %> <span class="tech-tag"><%= q[:type] %></span></h3>
        <button type="button" class="help-btn"
                data-jev-examples="<%= q[:key] %>"
                aria-label="Explain <%= q[:title] %>"
                title="Explain <%= q[:title] %>">?</button>
      </div>
      <p class="jev-question"><%= q[:blurb] %></p>
      <p class="jev-example-count"><%= pluralize(q[:examples].size, "example") %> behind the ?</p>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <div class="card jev-section">
    <div class="card-head">
      <h3>The verdict</h3>
      <button type="button" class="help-btn"
              data-jev-examples="verdict"
              aria-label="Explain The verdict"
              title="Explain The verdict">?</button>
    </div>
    <p class="jev-question">Typed answers fold into a thresholded verdict. Run by hand the annotator writes it as a <span class="mono">&lt;stem&gt;.jev.json</span> sidecar beside the manifest; through this runner it comes back in the response body, because the job directory is not the runner's to write in.</p>
    <div class="log-console jev-verdict">
<pre>{
  "headline": "screen_explosion",
  "print_route": "process_halftone",
  "tonal_score": 0.95,
  "tonal_label": "Flat",
  "evidence_actionable": false,
  "low_confidence": ["tonal_structure"],
  "confidence_floor": 0.6
}</pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card jev-section">
    <div class="card-head">
      <h3>Confidence rules</h3>
      <button type="button" class="help-btn"
              data-jev-examples="confidence"
              aria-label="Explain Confidence rules"
              title="Explain Confidence rules">?</button>
    </div>
    <p class="jev-question">Certainty is derived differently per type: a choice carries it, a score does not, a noul measures it against a coin flip.</p>
    <p class="jev-example-count">3 rules behind the ?</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p class="jev-advisory">
  Jev is advisory only. The deterministic gates in the pipeline remain the source of truth;
  it never ranks candidates and never picks a winner. On a proof page, the
  <strong>Ask Jev</strong> button triggers the run through the runner.
</p>

<%#
  EXAMPLE POPUPS. One per section, opened by that section's "?" through the same
  CsModal the colour map and the palettes library use, so the page has ONE modal
  rather than several bespoke overlays with several close behaviours.
%>
<% questions.each do |q| %>
  <template id="jevExamples-<%= q[:key] %>">
    <div class="jev-popup">
      <p class="jev-popup-q"><%= q[:blurb] %></p>
      <div class="jev-popup-list">
        <% q[:examples].each do |name, text| %>
          <div class="jev-card">
            <div class="q"><%= name %></div>
            <div class="a"><%= text %></div>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>
  </template>
<% end %>

<template id="jevExamples-verdict">
  <div class="jev-popup">
    <p class="jev-popup-q">Typed answers fold into a thresholded verdict, and the verdict stays advisory — it never overrides a gate.</p>
    <div class="jev-popup-list">
      <div class="jev-card"><div class="q">low_confidence</div><div class="a">Answer under the floor</div></div>
      <div class="jev-card"><div class="q">confidence_floor</div><div class="a">0.6 — below it, unresolved</div></div>
      <div class="jev-card"><div class="q">tonal_label</div><div class="a">The score, named</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>

<template id="jevExamples-confidence">
  <div class="jev-popup">
    <p class="jev-popup-q">Certainty is derived differently per question type.</p>
    <div class="jev-popup-list">
      <div class="jev-card"><div class="q">choice</div><div class="a">Used directly</div></div>
      <div class="jev-card"><div class="q">score</div><div class="a">Always 0.0 — uses max(probabilities)</div></div>
      <div class="jev-card"><div class="q">noul</div><div class="a">Certainty is 2·|noul − 0.5|</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>

<%#
  THE CONF PREVIEW. Terminal-styled and EDITABLE, in the same spirit as the JSON
  page's generated-spec popup: the <textarea> is the live target, validated on
  every keystroke.

  It mirrors chopshop-jev's QUESTIONS + CONFIDENCE_FLOOR exactly. The important
  caveat is stated IN the popup rather than left to be discovered: the annotator
  has no conf input, so this previews and validates but does not change what is
  asked. See JevConf.
%>
<template id="jevConfTemplate">
  <div class="jev-conf">
    <p class="jev-conf-warn">
      <strong>Preview only.</strong> The annotator asks
      <span class="mono">jev_annotate.py:QUESTIONS</span>, a module constant with no
      <span class="mono">--questions</span> flag — so an edit here does not yet change what Jev is
      asked. It is validated, and it is the exact shape a
      <span class="mono">--questions</span> flag would take.
    </p>

    <div class="jev-conf-head">
      <span class="jev-conf-label">metrics</span>
      <span class="jev-conf-metrics-note">the deterministic numbers each mode is measured against</span>
    </div>
    <ul class="jev-conf-metric-list">
      <% JevConf::METRICS.each do |m| %>
        <li class="mono"><%= m %></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>

    <div class="jev-conf-editor">
      <div class="jev-conf-gutter" aria-hidden="true">json</div>
      <textarea id="jevConfText" class="mono" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off"
                aria-label="Jev configuration"><%= h(JevConf.default_json) %></textarea>
    </div>

    <p class="jev-conf-status" id="jevConfStatus" role="status"></p>

    <div class="jev-conf-actions">
      <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm" id="jevConfReset">Run default</button>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm" id="jevConfApply">Validate</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
  // ---------- Jev: example popups + the conf preview ----------
  //
  // One delegated listener for both, so it works for content injected into the
  // modal after it opens.
  (function () {
    var modal = window.CsModal;

    // Validate on every keystroke. A silently-rejected edit is how you end up
    // running something you did not write, so the error sits beside the box.
    function checkConf(ta, st) {
      var out;
      try {
        var parsed = JSON.parse(ta.value);
        if (!parsed || typeof parsed !== 'object' || Array.isArray(parsed)) {
          throw new Error('the conf must be a JSON object');
        }
        if (!parsed.questions || typeof parsed.questions !== 'object') {
          throw new Error('the conf needs a "questions" object');
        }
        if (!Object.keys(parsed.questions).length) {
          throw new Error('"questions" cannot be empty -- Jev would be asked nothing');
        }
        out = Object.keys(parsed.questions).length + ' questions, valid JSON';
      } catch (err) {
        out = 'invalid JSON: ' + err.message;
      }
      st.textContent = out;
      st.className = 'jev-conf-status' + (out.indexOf('invalid') === 0 ? ' is-bad' : ' is-ok');
    }

    document.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
      var help = e.target.closest('.help-btn[data-jev-examples]');
      if (help) {
        e.preventDefault();
        var key = help.dataset.jevExamples;
        var tpl = document.getElementById('jevExamples-' + key);
        if (!modal || !tpl) return;
        modal.open(help.getAttribute('aria-label').replace(/^Explain /, ''), tpl.innerHTML);
        return;
      }

      if (e.target.closest('#jevConfBtn')) {
        e.preventDefault();
        var ctpl = document.getElementById('jevConfTemplate');
        if (!modal || !ctpl) return;
        modal.open('Jev configuration', ctpl.innerHTML);
        var ta = document.getElementById('jevConfText');
        var st = document.getElementById('jevConfStatus');
        if (!ta || !st) return;
        var defaults = ta.value;
        ta.addEventListener('input', function () { checkConf(ta, st); });
        checkConf(ta, st);

        var reset = document.getElementById('jevConfReset');
        if (reset) {
          reset.addEventListener('click', function () {
            ta.value = defaults;
            checkConf(ta, st);
            ta.focus();
          });
        }
        var apply = document.getElementById('jevConfApply');
        if (apply) {
          apply.addEventListener('click', function () { ta.focus(); checkConf(ta, st); });
        }
      }
    });
  })();
</script>
